# NOTE: .gitignore is never consulted for a file git already tracks. If ignored
# files keep showing up as modified, they were committed at some point — the fix
# is `git rm -r --cached <path>`, not another pattern here. That is what happened
# to .venv/ and __pycache__/, both untracked on 2026-08-12.

__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.venv/

app/frontend/node_modules/
app/frontend/dist/
app/frontend/*.tsbuildinfo
app/data/
ui/node_modules/
blpl/webapp/static/assets/
.env
# ...but the example must be committed. A personal ~/.gitignore with `.env.*` in
# it is a common and sensible thing to have, and it silently swallows this file —
# a repo-level rule outranks core.excludesFile, so state it here rather than
# expecting every clone to have the same global config.
!.env.example
# Clerk writes app/frontend/.env.local with the publishable key AND the
# secret key. Covered by the .env rule only if your personal ~/.gitignore
# happens to have `.env.*`; state it here so no clone depends on that.
.env.local
*.env.local
*.swp
blpl-db

# Vendor documents belong to a project, not to the app source tree. Two
# `git add -A` runs from this directory swept in 61 MB of them — five
# datasheets, a 34 MB footprint archive, and the __MACOSX resource forks that
# came out of it — none of it referenced by anything, all of it permanent in
# every clone and checkout. The tests build their own PDFs.
app/*.pdf
app/*.txt
app/*.zip

# macOS writes these into any directory it touches — and into any archive it
# creates. Nothing reads them, on any operating system, including macOS: the
# __MACOSX tree is a resource fork for a filesystem that has not needed one
# since Mac OS 9, and .DS_Store is a folder's window position. 78 of them came
# out of one vendor zip and into this repository.
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
__MACOSX/
.AppleDouble
.AppleDB
.AppleDesktop
.LSOverride
.Spotlight-V100
.TemporaryItems
.Trashes
.VolumeIcon.icns
.fseventsd
.apdisk
.com.apple.timemachine.donotpresent

# Diagram reference material, for people rather than for builds. The standards
# the block-diagram shape rules were read against — IEC 60617, IEEE 315-1975,
# ANSI/IEEE 91-1991 — are 96 MB on disk and 78 MB in the pack. They are opened
# when those rules are revisited and read by nothing that builds, so they stay
# on the machine of whoever is doing that work.
#
# Named one by one, rather than ignoring the folder and re-admitting the
# exceptions. That shape is a trap here: `customization-inspirations/*` excludes
# the *directories* too, and git will not re-include a file inside an excluded
# directory, so a later `!.../still-needed.css` would silently do nothing and
# the file would go missing with no rule appearing to be at fault.
#
# Listing what leaves also means anything added here is kept by default, which
# is the safer way round. A file that should have been ignored costs one line;
# a file that was silently ignored costs the work that produced it.
#
# Everything not named below stays tracked — including tailwind-colors.css,
# which palette.py reads to generate the theme, and the two symbol-library
# submodules, which cost a pointer each.
mermaid/customization-inspirations/electronics-symbols/
mermaid/customization-inspirations/Electrical_symbols_library.svg
